{"id": "southeast_asia_friendslop_and_30m", "source_key": "thegamebusiness", "source": "thegamebusiness.com", "section": "deep_analysis", "title": "Southeast Asia, friendslop and $30m video games are taking over the industry. Or are they? – Here’s The Dring", "url": "https://www.thegamebusiness.com/p/southeast-asia-friendslop-and-30m", "published_at": "2026-09-07T17:22:19", "author": "Christopher Dring", "excerpt": "This week, we tackle the thorny issue of video game industry trends", "text": "Playback speed\n×\nSubtitles\nShare post\nShare post at current time\n0:00\n/\nPaid episode\nThe full episode is only available to paid subscribers of The Game Business\nSoutheast Asia, friendslop and $30m video games are taking over the industry. Or are they? – Here’s The Dring\nThis week, we tackle the thorny issue of video game industry trends\nChristopher Dring\nSep 07, 2026\n∙ Paid\nHello there!\nOver, the last week, I’ve read far too many opinions on the future direction of the video game industry. It’s all Roblox. Or Steam. It’s definitely not console. It’s certainly not live service. It’s mostly China, and a bit of Korea. The jobs are all leaving California and going to India. Kids don’t want pretty graphics. Or long games. And the video game media is finished.\nNow, whenever I read those things, there’s a little voice in my brain that goes ‘yeah, but…’\nSo, this week, I am going to be talking about trends. Plus, rounding up some big stories, including Xbox Game Pass’s new streaming caps and PlayStation’s latest State of Play.\nLet’s go!\nThe industry loves a trend.\nOf course it does. Investors like to back businesses that are capitalizing on the new ‘thing’. And tapping into that can be the difference between finding an audience and disappearing without a trace.\nFor a good few years, indie publishers would tell me that their job is to ‘put games in the best position to get lucky’. Luck isn’t the best business strategy, but there’s a truth to it. You can say the right things, tick all the marketing boxes, make a brilliant game, and still fail to make any money. And in 2026, that’s almost as true for AAA games as it is for indie projects.\nTherefore, if there’s a trend (whether that’s a game trend, a business trend, or a marketing trend) that can increase your chances of success, then you better chase it. Quick, everyone make a Souls-like or an extraction shooter. What do you mean your game’s not free-to-play? Or $30? Where’s your co-op mode? Why haven’t you set-up a Discord channel yet?\nThe Game Business Show\nChristopher Dring hosts interviews, in-depth analysis and special features on what's truly going on in the video games industry.\nChristopher Dring hosts interviews, in-depth analysis and special features on what's truly going on in the video games industry.\nListen on\nSubstack App\nApple Podcasts\nSpotify\nYouTube\nAppears in episode\nChristopher Dring\nRecent Episodes\nGTA 6 reveal causes Xbox and PlayStation sales to jump by a third\nSep 3   •   Christopher Dring\nCD Projekt Red: If you want a cost-driven development, you cannot make The Witcher 4\nSep 1   •   Christopher Dring\nHow a legendary UK comic ended up in a video game – Here’s The Dring\nAug 31   •   Christopher Dring\nAssassin’s Creed creator Patrice Désilets: “I have two or three games left”\nAug 28   •   Christopher Dring\nUnity CEO: “The cost of building a game is coming down markedly”\nAug 25   •   Christopher Dring\nVideo game consoles will bounce back – Here’s The Dring\nAug 25   •   Christopher Dring\nThe video games most at risk from PlayStation’s physical switch-off\nAug 20   •   Christopher Dring", "html": "<div class=\"visibility-check\"></div><div><div class=\"shows-video-player-container container-dlhqPD\"><div class=\"playerRoot-b3mviO\"><div class=\"shows-video-player-shell-area shellArea-Vp0r0v\"><div><div class=\"fullscreen\"><div class=\"playerShell-T21pqv\" style=\"--player-aspect-ratio: 1.7777777777777777;\"><div class=\"stage-dto3lX\">